A||||8||BAM-BAM||3.4L - what would it take to get 200HP?||||1||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||01-29-2001||08:32 PM|||| I am getting ready to do the engine swap thing and I'm torn in two directions. I want the power and asthetics of the 3800sc with the afforability of the 3.4L. Yet I would like to have a sleeper(visualy stock) that would raise an eyebrow or two. I plan on doing most if not all the work myself, save any machine work,  that will be outsourced. Is it relatively easy to reach 200hp with the 3.4L? or would I be better off just going with the 3800sc? I know that the 3.4 would be far easier, and if the power is there it might be the better way. I would add a turbo later also. Some one here must know the answers to these questions. Cost is a relative concerne, but I won't be on that tight of a budget. LMK and TIA<BR> <BR>  Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Don B. <BR>85GT<BR>Eibach/KYB Susp.<BR>Mr.Mikes 500 series seats,headliner! MOMO Commando Steering Wheel, White Face Race Style Gauges And Soon To Be More!!! YEAH BABY-YEAH||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||01-29-2001||09:34 PM||||I am building a 210 horsepower 3.4-liter Camaro engine for my Formula. How do I know that you ask? I used Dyno2000 to come up with that figure. <P>I have 9.7-1 compression<BR>Ported heads, had the combustion chambers balanced and had SI stainless steel valves fitted with a performance valve job.<BR>Sharp edges removed from the combustion chambers  <BR>Heads shaved .008” and flow tested, they are good for about 230 horsepower modified like I have them according to Aeric High Performance<BR>Block zero decked and squared<BR>ARP rod, main studs, GM bolts are cheap junk<BR>Block bored .020” <BR>Engine assembly fully balanced<BR>Gasket matched intake manifolds<BR>Polished throttle body and gasket matched<BR>FOCOA headers<BR>Fieroshop aluminum flywheel and kevlar clutch<BR>Custom chip<BR>Crane roller rocker arms with correct valve train geometry although you don’t need them. They are only good for about 5 horsepower. To late to return them<BR>Borla cat back exhaust system<BR>GM performance 12363220 camshaft with matching springs and retainers (great camshaft)<BR>Cloyes double roller timing chain set<BR>Underdrive pulley (every little bit helps)<P>That was off the top of my head. The key components are the headers, exhaust system, camshaft, and heads. Those are the parts that will get you 200 horsepower or more. <P>Using Dragstrip2000 my 2925 lb. Formula (including driver) will go 0-60 mph in 5.5 seconds and run the quarter mile in 13.8 seconds at 99 mph with the five-speed transmission. If it does that I will be very happy. Next year the Nitrous will come. <P> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||01-29-2001||09:38 PM||||In stock form the 3.4-liter engine will have 166 horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BAM-BAM||01-29-2001||10:12 PM|||| Thanks alot! Keep the good info coming. Is your TB bored also? How about ignition modules and injectors and such things as that. I have the Borla cat back waiting in the garage. Who did the custom chip and how much? I need some details. Thanks  for this much but I NEED MORE!!!!!<P> Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
